index 5ae3259..c949e01 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -129,3 +129,147 @@ This codebase builds upon:
 ## License
 
 This project is licensed under the MIT License - see the [LICENSE](LICENSE) file for details.
+
+## Minimal WiMF adaptation
+
+This branch starts from upstream WFM commit
+`e01ab1c193eec2264a01fd3211c16fe931b701e8`. WFM remains the default
+(`--method wfm`). Both methods use the existing trainer, sampler, BraTS loader,
+Haar transforms (including LLL / 3), 32-input/8-output UNet, modality labels,
+TensorBoard tags, exports and `scripts/evaluate.py`.
+
+WiMF implements the **boundary-velocity, no-CFG variant** of Section 4.1 /
+Algorithm 1 of [Improved Mean Flows, v2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.02012v2).
+We checked Appendix A and the
+[official implementation at bf60cd7](https://github.com/Lyy-iiis/imeanflow/blob/bf60cd7cb653f6628e59d48034b333c5eba445e2/imf.py).
+This is an adaptation to WFM's conditional wavelet UNet, rather than a
+reproduction of the official ImageNet DiT architecture and training recipe.
+
+### Equations and explicit choices
+
+Let W be WFM's scaled Haar transform, x = W(target), C the concatenation of
+the three observed modalities' coefficients, and m their coefficient mean.
+The target is excluded from C and m. A fixed condition C and target label y
+are held constant through the JVP. Tensor shapes are x, m, e, z: `[B,8,H,W,D]`,
+C: `[B,24,H,W,D]`, and r, t, h, y: `[B]`.
+
+```text
+e = m + prior_sigma * W(image-space standard Gaussian noise)
+z = (1-t)*x + t*e                  # target at t=0; informed prior at t=1
+v_target = e-x                    # opposite sign to WFM's forward velocity
+h = t-r
+fn(z,r,t) = UNet([z,C], 1000*t, y, intervals=h)
+v_pred = fn(z,t,t)                 # learned boundary velocity, NOT e-x
+D = JVP(fn, (z,r,t), (v_pred,0,1))
+V = fn(z,r,t) + (t-r)*stop_gradient(D)
+loss = mean((V-v_target)^2)        # average batch, 8 bands and spatial axes
+x_sample = e - fn(e,0,1)           # exactly one network evaluation
+```
+
+`1000` above is the default `diffusion_steps`; scaling is inside `fn`, so
+forward AD includes its chain rule. The new interval uses physical **unit
+scale**, not `1000*h`. The extra two-layer MLP is added to the existing time
+embedding; its final layer starts at zero. It is initialized after the original
+backbone, without consuming the loader's RNG state. The default network gains
+82,432 parameters: 81,512,072 (WFM) → 81,594,504 (WiMF).
+The official DiT uses h-only time conditioning; retaining WFM's absolute-time
+branch and adding h is an explicit backbone-preserving conditioning adaptation.
+
+WiMF samples two independent sigmoid-normal times with mean -0.4 and std 1,
+sorts them into r ≤ t, and sets the first `floor(B/2)` rows to r=t, matching
+the official sampler. Physical time runs opposite to WFM's source-to-target
+clock. The change to continuous intervals is part of learning mean velocities.
+
+WFM's interior bridge noise is removed: the straight path has derivative
+exactly e-x. Retaining a bridge term a(t)*noise would instead require an
+additional a'(t)*noise velocity target and a corresponding path derivation;
+that is not Algorithm 1's path. `sigma_max` remains a WFM bridge setting and
+has no effect in WiMF. Optional endpoint noise is a different distribution.
+
+**`wimf_prior_sigma=0` preserves WFM's deterministic informed endpoint.**
+This departs from official iMF's independent standard Gaussian prior. It is
+an explicit comparison setting, not a validated fix for the previous run:
+with a known deterministic endpoint, interior velocities can be recovered
+algebraically and endpoint quality may still be poor. Positive sigma is an
+optional stochastic informed-prior experiment; choose it explicitly and use
+the same value for training and sampling. Noise is transformed in image space
+so it follows WFM's scaled wavelet covariance. No noise value is claimed to
+guarantee convergence.
+
+We keep **plain coefficient-mean L2**, as in WFM and the paper's Figure 3.
+Algorithm 1 leaves `metric` open; the official recipe instead applies adaptive
+weighting to a per-example *sum*. We do not copy that weighting or silently
+change the loss scale. We also omit the optional auxiliary v-head, CFG/dropout
+and in-context DiT conditioning. The JVP and boundary prediction run under
+`no_grad`; forward differentiation still computes D. A separate ordinary
+prediction retains parameter gradients, giving the specified stopped-JVP
+gradient without saving an unnecessary higher-order graph.
+
+### Running and comparison settings
+
+Use the original commands above with `--method wimf --wimf_prior_sigma 0.0`.
+For sampling, also pass **`--sampling_steps 1`**; other step counts are rejected.
+WiMF checkpoints use `wimf_unified_*.pt` and `opt_wimf_*.pt`; WFM names stay
+unchanged. Start fresh: checkpoints from the previous WiMF implementation have
+different time-feature semantics and are not a continuation of this method.
+
+All original training defaults remain: AdamW with betas (0.9,0.999), epsilon
+1e-8, weight decay 0, LR 1e-5, batch 4, global gradient clipping at 1, float32,
+no warmup/schedule/EMA, shuffled DataLoader with 8 workers and `drop_last=True`.
+The CLI's original budget is 200,000; the README comparison command explicitly
+chooses 50,000. Seeds, preprocessing, data discovery/order policy, modality
+selection, logging intervals and evaluation are unchanged. Initial loader RNG
+is preserved. On CUDA, flow draws use the separate CUDA generator and do not
+advance the CPU loader generator: a two-epoch check matched subject orders for
+both methods. On CPU, flow and loader draws share a generator, so later epoch
+orders can diverge. Method-specific time/noise draws and predictions differ.
+
+WiMF's `training_mixture/` image cards are **training estimates** containing
+target information: IDWT(z-t*V) uses the relation E[x|z,C] = z-t*E[e-x|z,C].
+The wavelet cards in that group show predicted reverse-time mean velocity u.
+WFM's existing image tags and behavior remain unchanged; `loss/MSE` and band
+MSE retain their coefficient-mean meanings for both methods.
+
+WiMF additionally monitors **actual prior-only 1-NFE generation** on one fixed
+training subject (dataset index 0), at the first update and every 200 updates
+when TensorBoard is enabled. It reuses the sampling script, with no target
+pixels in the model input. Unified training checks all four missing modalities.
+The `one_step/train/` cards compare ground truth, the deterministic mean prior,
+and generation. Full-volume MSE/SSIM and foreground MSE are scored against both
+generation and the mean prior, using the original evaluator's normalization,
+155-slice crop and scoring functions. SSIM remains full-volume, including in
+the foreground metric routine. The mean-prior reference is deterministic even
+if optional endpoint noise is enabled. Fixed monitoring noise, restored RNG
+state and restored model modes keep monitoring separate from optimization.
+This single training case is a regression diagnostic, **not held-out validation**.
+Monitoring adds time to wall-clock throughput, not the `time/forward` update
+measurement. Evaluate held-out 1-NFE exports with the unchanged evaluator.
+
+One shared sampler correction unwraps DataLoader's `['none']` missing-modality
+value before checking it; upstream otherwise skips complete subjects. Saving
+and evaluator behavior are otherwise unchanged, including cropped arrays and
+identity output affines. Checkpoint/resume retains WFM's separate raw model /
+optimizer files and its existing limitations: no saved data/RNG cursor or
+method configuration, and optimizer lookup uses the current logging directory.
+Supply consistent prior and model settings when resuming. WiMF supports the
+existing single-device, float32 MRI configuration without attention, activation
+checkpointing or dropout; unsupported forward-AD configurations are rejected.
+WiMF requires PyTorch with `torch.func.jvp`; checks used the existing PyTorch
+2.11 environment. The original dependency files remain unchanged.
+
+Focused checks (using the existing environment):
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q tests/test_wimf_minimal.py
+```
+
+These check equations, time/interval derivatives, stopped gradients, loss
+reduction, endpoint consistency, WFM initialization parity, a small 3D
+forward/backward and exactly one sampling evaluation, plus prior-only monitoring
+and its state isolation. A separate B200 check used four real BraTS subjects at
+`[4,1,224,224,160]` per modality with the full backbone, original AdamW settings,
+float32 and native backend flags (matmul TF32 off, cuDNN TF32 on). Two isolated
+updates passed with finite gradients: peak allocated memory 72.03 GiB, reserved
+85.48 GiB. Full-batch generation passed with one network call. These checks do
+not establish reconstruction quality or convergence. No full training run was
+started and no checkpoint was written by these checks.
